A||||3||cscougar||Cold Start Problem||||1||
Z||000000||cscougar||02-01-2000||10:56 PM||||I just rebuilt a 1985 2m6.  The engines runs perfectly when warmed up but is hard to start when cold and when started, it spits and sputters and backfires some until it warms up.  What should I look for?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||ITA_FIERO||02-01-2000||11:21 PM||||My 86SE was doing similar (poor cold starting) and it was simply a bad fuel pump relay.  There are two small relays (roughly 1 inch cube looking deals) on the firewall immediately behind the driver's seat.  If my memory serves me correctly it's the one on the right (as you stand behind the car looking forward).  It's function is to run the fuel pump for a few seconds when you first turn on the ignition and prior to actually getting the motor started.  It's sort of a "head start" on building fuel pressure to assist in cold starting.  After a few seconds, the relay switches off and the fuel pump is operated through the oil pump relay. So after car is warm, or in warm conditions, you may not even notice if the relay was bad.  Just a thought (and very easy fix if that's it).<P>PS  Geez I'm long winded!<p>[This message has been edited by ITA_FIERO (edited 02-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||02-02-2000||12:08 AM||||Maybe you should be looking for a home in a warmer climate. That would solve your COLD start problems. Haha, I beat you to the punch, Monkey!||||reg||1
Z||000003||AL||02-02-2000||12:15 AM||||hey cougar, if that's not it check your cold start injectors, if they don't shoot the  fuel right guess what hard cold starts. ||||reg||1
